<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Sforza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1466">1466</a>–<a href="/years/1510">1510</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sforza">House of Sforza</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Sforza was lord of Pesaro from 1483 and the first husband of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucrezia-borgia">Lucrezia Borgia</a>, whom he married in 1493. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a> had the marriage annulled in 1497, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> drove Giovanni from Pesaro in 1500. He returned after the pope's death in 1503 and ruled until his own death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-sforza.jpg" alt="Giovanni Sforza" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pesarese 15th Century · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pesarese_15th_Century,_Giovanni_Sforza,_1466-1510,_Lord_of_Pesaro_(obverse),_15th_century,_NGA_45487.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Giovanni Sforza was born on 5 July 1466, the illegitimate son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costanzo-i-sforza">Costanzo I Sforza</a>, of the Pesaro and Gradara line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sforza">House of Sforza</a>, while another branch held the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. When his father died in 1483 he inherited Pesaro and Gradara at seventeen, and his father's widow Camilla d'Aragona at first ruled as regent. In 1489 he married Maddalena Gonzaga, daughter of Federico I of Mantua, who died the next year. As a link to Milan he then became valuable to the Borgia family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With the help of his cousin Cardinal Ascanio Sforza, the marriage with the twelve-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucrezia-borgia">Lucrezia Borgia</a> was agreed in February 1492, with a dowry of 31,000 ducats and a proxy wedding on 12 June. The couple spent two years in Pesaro, where Lucrezia never settled, and Giovanni's value to the Borgias faded as they turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a> found that he was spying for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1495, he left on campaign and in February 1497 fled the city in disguise.</p><p>Pressed by the pope and by Ascanio Sforza, he at first refused the annulment, which would have cost him the dowry and required him to declare himself impotent. He answered with the charge that Lucrezia had committed incest with her father and later her brothers, a claim that has shadowed the family ever since though it cannot be verified. When his own family threatened to withdraw its protection, he accepted terms that let him keep the dowry but still declared his impotence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was excommunicated in 1500, survived an attempt on his life by the citizens of one of his towns, and appealed in vain to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a>. After the death of Alexander VI and Cesare's illness in 1503 he went back, and the following year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> confirmed him as vicar of Pesaro. With his second wife, Ginevra Tiepolo, he had two sons, Ascanio, who died in infancy, and Costanzo II, who succeeded him. Giovanni died in Pesaro on 27 July 1510, aged 44.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giovanni Sforza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Giovanni Sforza</b>, Costanzo's son, married Lucrezia Borgia, but the marriage was annulled and Cesare Borgia expelled him in 1500.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-pesaro">Lordship of Pesaro</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pesaro, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Pesaro, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.91,12.91&amp;year=1494" aria-label="Pesaro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Giovanni Sforza"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5789 3777h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5789 3777h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.91,12.91&amp;year=1494">What was here in 1494? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
